\f1\fs22 That evening, Chanta joined Sarah and Kathy for dinner. Mouth agape, he took a seat on one long side of the table while Sarah presided at the head and Kathy sat opposite him on the other side. The moment Chanta settled in, Kathy seemed to vanish behind the towering sprawling feast.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Castles and moats of sugar, fat, and calories loomed across the table\'92s length and width, massed atop unopened casserole dishes, battered pots, mystery jars, and doomed takeaway bags. A rock-pile of Fool\'92s Gold sandwiches heaped at all angles on a plate the size of a bird bath\'97bacon, peanut butter, and banana mash oozing from each slab of sourdough. Down the table\'92s centre, an equally imposing mound of hot dogs and sauerkraut squatted with the gravity of a medieval fortress. To Chanta\'92s immediate right, meatballs steamed like a peat bog; beyond Sarah at the table\'92s head, four huge pitchers of black cherry pop fizzed like disco mirror balls. There was a mountain of mash that would've had Richard Dreyfuss demanding engineers to model Devils Tower. A teetering stack of pineapple and pecan cake loomed between Chanta and the now-invisible Kathy. Between them towered a Christmas-tree sized croquembouche, its choux puffs bound in golden caramel like an edible fortress wall. There was more\'97too much to name without triggering acid reflux: \'91Just Pretend\'92 Harvard Texas Caviar, Spanish omelettes, fried pickles, sugar-glazed salmon, potatoes the size of cannon balls; banana pudding in a peanut-butter-and-bacon cookie crust, topped with an avalanche of cream; a meatloaf the size of a large cat. Chanta worried there could still be a large cat, somewhere under the pile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Road-kill pie?\'94 Sarah offered, presenting a segment of mostly pastry, gravy, and best-left-unquestioned meat.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Uh, no, thanks. Where did all this come from?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Offerings from the Elvi devotees,\'94 said Denver Elvis, materialising by the parlour door, unnoticed until now. \'93For the Holy Momma. And our talented songwriter.\'94 Denver smiled with greasy pride.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I wish you wouldn\'92t call me that,\'94 Sarah said, glaring at him, \'93or him that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What, talented?\'94 Chanta blinked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He\'92s setting aside years of great squabbling over the lyrics,\'94 Denver said in a way that was meant to sound biblical.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta lifted a bowl, peered in as if facing a stool sample. \'93What the tax even is this?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Captain Denver leaned in for a look. \'93Oh, one of my momma\'92s favourites. Mushrooms. She called it Viva Las Fungus.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Is there a cheeseburger in here somewhere?\'94 Chanta asked with honest desperation. From Kathy\'92s side of the table, a column of burgers angled into the light. Chanta grabbed and demolished one.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the tax is this?\'94 Kathy called from the far end of Coronary Mountain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah held up a small dish, analysing it. \'93Coffee grounds and bacon fat,\'94 she explained, then snatched it, ladling black gobs into her mouth with conviction. \'93What?\'94 she snapped at Chanta\'92s horror. \'93I\'92ve got cravings. This parasite is sucking the life out of me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Denver, unfazed, loudly flicked pages in his notebook and scribbled like an officious police secretary.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Get rid of it,\'94 Kathy said, the words swimming through a fog of edible debris.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s face shifted, a compliance Chanta had never seen. \'93Abortions are for animals and the Cloudborne,\'94 she recited, voice dead-eyed. \'93An Airborne respects the sanctity of life.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, right,\'94 Kathy said. \'93The higher the Airborne, the more scratch marks on her cervix.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah recoiled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Surely you won\'92t take Little Clinton away from\'85 us,\'94 Chanta pleaded. Suddenly, his anxiety cooled; he imagined a halo glowing around Sarah\'92s belly, and heard in his mind the kind of inspired lyrics that slay tyrants and dissolve corruption. For a moment, he was sure Little Clinton could prevail. He\'92d build whole worlds in song, out of hope.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93She was raped,\'94 said a Christmas-tree sized croquembouche with Kathy\'92s voice.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, 
\f2\i raped
\f1\i0  is a bit strong, isn\'92t it?\'94 Chanta tried.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Typical man!\'94 accused the mutant 
\f2\i choux
\f1\i0  puffs. \'93Always minimising violence against women.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah glared. \'93They drugged me, then\'85 
