\f1\i0 \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, but you gotta admit the drugs were great,\'94 Chanta said, looking for light in the ruins.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Were they?\'94 asked Croquembouche-Kathy, her tone all French patisserie and withering incredulity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He could be a mutant. Hideous,\'94 Sarah muttered. As if ugliness was an existential threat just beneath nuclear war\'97or worse, a basket of muffins from Alexander Shakti.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What are you talking about?\'94 Chanta said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93There\'92s synthetic DNA attached to the child\'92s DNA,\'94 croquembouche-Kathy intoned. \'93I don\'92t know what he\'92ll be.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s face cinched tight; she looked at the floor, somewhere far off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Come on\'97the Elvi worship you. You\'92re safe here. What\'92s the harm in giving them one little baby?\'94 Chanta trembled as he contemplated the abject loneliness of a world without Little Clinton and the inspiration he brought, as well as the respect of the Elvi. Chanta had never been respected before.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Seems like it\'92s the men doing the campaigning for the little monster,\'94 Croquembouche-Kathy said. \'93They never credit women for carrying children, for the discrimination, the endless pile-on. No one says it to your face, but you\'92ll see it for yourself\'97every day. When they\'92re not trying to fuck you or kill you, men are really very funny.\'94 Kathy wasn\'92t laughing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta suspected\'97only half-facetious now\'97that the croquembouche\'92s bitterness came from hard experience: a working-mother-pastry in a man-pastry\'92s world.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah slumped in her chair, the old world crowding in\'97a professional\'92s freedom, everyone at her heels. Tax! \'93Maybe I should get rid of it,\'94 she murmured, barely above the clatter of plates.
